      <h1>{frontmatter.title}</h1>
      <time>{frontmatter.date}</time>

      <p class="intro">Wingspan vs Everdell comes down to one question. Do you want an engine you can watch grow, or a city you can point at when it is done? Both are gorgeous engine builders. Both cost between $55 and $75. Both will eat an hour of your night. They are also less alike than they look sitting next to each other on a shelf.</p>

      <p>I own both. I have played Wingspan more than 60 times and Everdell about 25. Here is the part most comparisons skip. The heavier game is not the one my group asks for on a weeknight. Weight does not decide which box gets opened.</p>

      <p>Below is the honest breakdown. How each game actually feels, where each one wins, the one real flaw each has, and a third game to consider if neither lands for your table.</p>

      <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
      <p>If you want one line with no scrolling, here it is. Pick Wingspan if your table is often 2 people or 5 people, you want a game you can teach in 15 minutes, and a little luck in how you collect food does not bother you. Pick Everdell if you have exactly 3 or 4 players, you want zero dice, and your group enjoys a heavier puzzle with more to track.</p>

      <p>If you want the reasons, keep reading. The reasons are where the surprise lives, and the surprise is not which game is smarter.</p>

      <h2>What an Engine Builder Actually Needs</h2>
      <p>Here is the key insight. An engine builder lives or dies on one feeling: growth. Turn one you can barely afford anything. Turn twenty you are doing three things at once and it is all your doing. Both of these games nail that feeling, and they both do it with cards laid out in front of you. Where they split is everything around the growth.</p>

      <p>The first test is downtime. At a full table, a worker placement game makes you wait. Everdell punishes a slow table because four people are all reaching for the same small pile of berries and twigs. Wingspan is gentler. You activate one habitat, take your thing, pass the turn. Turns come back fast even with five players.</p>

      <p>The second test is teaching cost. Wingspan takes about 15 minutes to teach. Everdell takes 30 to 40, and it does not fully click until the first season change. That gap matters more than any mechanic in either box, and it is the thing reviews rarely measure.</p>

      <h2>Two Numbers That Decide It For You</h2>
      <p>Pay attention to this number: 5. Wingspan plays 1 to 5 players. Everdell plays 1 to 4. If your group is regularly five people, the choice is already made. Wingspan is the only one of the two that fits everyone at the table without someone sitting out.</p>

      <p>The second number is 2. That is the count where Everdell quietly gets worse and Wingspan quietly gets better. Everdell has action spaces that only appear in a four-player game, so at two players a chunk of the board sits empty and the tension drops. Wingspan at two is a tight race over food, eggs, and the end-of-round goals.</p>

      <p>Here is why that matters. Most people buy one of these two and then play it with the same one or two people for a year. The game that works at your real player count beats the game that collects awards at four.</p>

