      <h2>The Difference Nobody Mentions</h2>
      <p>I thought I understood both games after two years of playing them. Then I looked at my own play log and counted which one actually got picked.</p>

      <p class="twist-box"><strong>Here is the contradiction.</strong> Every ranking puts Everdell higher on depth. BoardGameGeek rates it heavier, hobbyists call it the more serious game, and the praise is earned. Yet Wingspan has hit my table more than twice as often. The deeper game is not the game we replay.</p>

      <p>The reason is not luck and it is not theme. It is setup and teach time. When four friends show up on a Friday and one has never played either, Wingspan gets me to a real first turn in 15 minutes. Everdell gets me a lecture about the Ever Tree. Depth is a promise about game four. Teach time is a promise about game one, and game one decides whether there is ever a game four.</p>

      <p>That is the whole twist. Everdell is worth the lecture once, and it rewards a group that shows up every week. Wingspan is the one that survives a random Tuesday, which is most Tuesdays.</p>

      <h2>When Wingspan Wins</h2>
      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wingspan+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Wingspan</a> is a game about attracting birds to your wildlife preserve. You have three habitats, and each one does one job. The forest gets you food, the grassland lays eggs, the wetland draws cards. You play a bird by paying its food cost and eggs, then every bird to its left in that row fires off its power, left to right. That chain is the engine. A habitat you build in round one is still paying you in round four.</p>

      <p>The game runs four rounds, and each round gives you one fewer action than the last: 8, then 7, then 6, then 5. There are 170 illustrated bird cards in the box, each with a power pulled from the real habits of that species. It won the Kennerspiel des Jahres in 2019 and has sold more than 2.6 million copies, and it plays solo against an automa when nobody else is around.</p>

      <p>What I like most is how fast a beginner can look like a player. I taught this to my brother-in-law over coffee, and by round three he was chaining wetland birds to draw three cards a turn. He lost, but he lost by four points, and he asked for a rematch.</p>

      <p>The honest flaw is that Wingspan can feel like four people solving puzzles next to each other. Bird powers that steal from opponents exist, but they are rare. Add a little dice luck in the food supply and a competitive group can feel like the board is ignoring them. Full notes on the <a href="/game/wingspan">Wingspan page</a>.</p>

      <h2>When Everdell Wins</h2>
      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Everdell+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Everdell</a> is a worker placement game about building a woodland city. You start winter with 2 workers and 15 spaces for cards. Place a worker to gather twigs, resin, pebbles, or berries, then spend those to build constructions and recruit critters into your city. The clever part is the pairing. If a critter's matching construction is already in your city, you play that critter for free, and it only works once per building.</p>

      <p>Then the seasons carry you forward. Spring gives you a third worker and fires every green production card you own. Summer gives you a fourth worker. Autumn gives you two more workers, which brings you to six, and opens the journey spaces for a last grab at points. Hosting events and stacking prosperity cards decides most close games.</p>

      <p>My favorite part is the board. The river of resources is finite. When the twigs run out, they are gone until someone spends them. That small scarcity turns a cute game about forest animals into an actual fight, and the first time a friend took the last berry I needed, I felt it.</p>

      <p>The honest flaw is the table. Everdell is a monster. The Ever Tree stands two feet tall, every player spreads cards across a wide city board, and the resource piles claim the middle. On a small table it does not fit, and teaching four new players is a real evening project. If your group is casual and your table is a coffee table, this is a hard sell no matter how good the game is.</p>

      <h2>The Third Option: Wyrmspan</h2>
      <p>If you love Wingspan's loop but want more to chew on, look at <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wyrmspan+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Wyrmspan</a>. It is the dragon version of the same idea, built by Stonemaier with Wingspan's own designer Elizabeth Hargrave as developer and Connie Vogelmann as the lead designer. You excavate a cave system, walk an adventurer meeple down it to trigger the dragons already roosting there, and hatch young dragons that grow into stronger ones.</p>

      <p>It plays 1 to 5, runs about 90 minutes, and comes with 183 dragon cards, so the combos are deeper and the turns are longer. It costs about $59.99. If Wingspan already feels too light and Everdell's teach time scares your group, this is the middle path. It is my pick for a table of experienced players who still want that card-chaining high.</p>

      <h2>The Shelf Test</h2>
      <p>Here is the Shelf Test. Would this game survive 10 plays on a shelf that already holds 40 games? That is the only standard that counts. A box earns its spot by getting picked, not by being pretty or expensive, and both of these are very pretty and very expensive.</p>

      <p>Wingspan survives on teach speed and player count. It is the one I can hand to a stranger and start playing, and it works at two, three, or five. Everdell survives on payoff. Ten plays in, the seasons, the events, and the free-critter combos still surprise me, and a group that learns it together gets a game that keeps opening up.</p>

      <p>Ten plays is the bar. If I had to bet on which of these two boxes gets there first in your house, I would bet on the one your group can start in 15 minutes. That is Wingspan in most homes and Everdell in a committed weekly group.</p>

      <p class="cta">Here is the part that matters. For a first buy, grab <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wingspan+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Wingspan</a> at about $55 if you play with 2 or 5 people or with beginners. Grab <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Everdell+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Everdell</a> at about $62 if you have a steady group of 3 or 4 who want the heavier game. Both prices bounce around, so catch Wingspan under $52 and Everdell under $65 and you have done well. Browse the <a href="/games/strategy">full strategy lineup</a>, the <a href="/blog/best-strategy-board-games">best strategy games</a> for more of this weight class, or the <a href="/blog/best-solo-board-games">best solo games</a> if you play alone. New to Wingspan? The <a href="/blog/how-to-play-wingspan">how to play Wingspan</a> guide walks the whole first game in order.</p>

